The statement is the belief as it stands, in the present: one claim, declarative, understood on
its own by a reader who never saw these days. When the evidence shows the state changed, one dated
clause says what held before and until when. When it shows variation across several occasions,
the statement says that variation as it is. The evidence keeps the history of who said what when;
the statement keeps the claim.

Later evidence carries the more recent state of things, and {{ resource_user_data.first_name }}'s
own words carry more than an inference or a button press. What {{ resource_user_data.first_name }}
says about the state itself — what the time is, what the rule is, what is true now — is the new
state of the belief, even said once. What {{ resource_user_data.first_name }} reports about one
occasion is that occasion. A report of one occasion that went differently, evidence that confirms
the belief, or a question about it, leaves the belief as held: the outcome is unchanged and the
statement is the belief as held.
